The Security Council,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Recalling its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),
Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to comply with international humanitarian law, including the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,
Condemning the terrorist attacks by Hamas that took place in Israel starting on 7 October 2023 and the taking of hostages, and expressing grave concern for the safety and well-being of all civilians,
Expressing grave concern at the deterioration of the humanitarian situation in Gaza, including the impact on civilians, and stressing the need for full, rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access,
Reaffirming the right of all States to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations,
Stressing the need to prevent the conflict from spreading, and the importance of a two-State solution,
Welcoming the diplomatic efforts of the Secretary-General, regional leaders, and all those working to secure the release of hostages and to provide humanitarian assistance,
1. Condemns the terrorist attacks by Hamas that took place in Israel starting on 7 October 2023 and the taking of hostages;
2. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages;
3. Reaffirms the right of all States to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and in accordance with international humanitarian law;
4. Calls for humanitarian pauses to allow full, rapid, safe and unhindered access for humanitarian assistance and to enable the evacuation of civilians;
5. Calls on all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the protection of civilians, and to protect humanitarian workers, medical personnel, and civilian infrastructure;
6. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance to civilians in Gaza, including food, water, fuel, medical supplies, and other essential goods, and calls for the establishment of humanitarian corridors;
7. Urges all States to exercise maximum restraint and prevent further escalation and to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States in the region;
8. Calls for the provision of medical care and humanitarian assistance to all hostages and to ensure their safety and well-being;
9. Reaffirms the importance of a two-State solution and calls for the resumption of negotiations towards a just and lasting peace;
10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

