1. Condemns in the strongest terms the terrorist attacks by Hamas that took place in Israel starting on 7 October 2023 and the taking of hostages, and demands that Hamas cease all terrorist attacks against Israel;
2. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages;
3. Reaffirms the inherent right of all States, including Israel, to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and in accordance with international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precaution;
4. Calls for immediate, sustained humanitarian pauses to allow full, rapid, safe and unhindered access for humanitarian assistance and to enable the evacuation of civilians;
5. Calls on all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precaution, to protect all civilians, including Palestinian civilians, and to protect humanitarian workers, medical personnel, medical facilities, hospitals, schools, United Nations facilities and civilian infrastructure;
6. Reaffirms that all parties must protect civilians and comply with international humanitarian law, including the prohibition of forced displacement;
7. Condemns the use of civilians as human shields and the embedding of military assets in civilian infrastructure by Hamas and other armed groups, and demands that all parties take all feasible precautions to protect civilians;
8. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate the sustained, continuous, at-scale and unimpeded delivery of humanitarian assistance to civilians in Gaza, including food, water, fuel, medical supplies, and other essential goods, including through the opening of additional crossings and humanitarian corridors into and within Gaza, and calls for the restoration of water and electricity;
9. Urges all States to exercise maximum restraint and prevent further escalation and to respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States in the region;
10. Calls for the provision of medical care and humanitarian assistance to all hostages and to ensure their safety and well-being;
11. Reaffirms the importance of a two-State solution based on relevant United Nations resolutions and the principle of land for peace, and calls for the resumption of negotiations towards a just and lasting peace;
12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

